Durarara!! AU
Chapter 20 – Part 3 
Curtis sat up straighter on the couch, long black braids shifting against the gray hoodie as the implication caught up with him a step too late. The drowned phone. The tower. The suite. The restored line. Somewhere in all of that, Anri had not simply sent a couple of worried messages and gone home.
“…Wait. How long were you looking for me?”
The silence on her end lasted only a second, but it was enough to make the answer worse before it arrived.
“…More than a few hours,” Anri admitted, voice small and honest. “After the fight. After you walked away. I kept thinking I’d find you around the next corner, and then the calls stopped connecting at all.”
Curtis shut his eyes and tipped his head back against the cushion. The sigh that left him was pure self-reproach, rough around the edges.
“…Fuck. I’m so sorry, Anri-chan. I’ll make it up to you somehow.”
On the other end, Anri’s fingers tightened around the phone. The apology landed warmly enough to ease one knot and leave another untouched — the part of her that still saw him vanishing into the crowd with that defeated expression, and the part that now held his address only a few blocks away like a fragile correction.
“…You don’t have to make it into a grand debt,” she said quietly. “I just needed to know you were okay. Hearing your voice… and getting the address… that already does more than you think.” A short pause, softer but steady. “If you want to make it up to me, then just don’t treat disappearing as the first solution next time. Text one line. Come home and let the people who care aim at the right place.”
Another quieter beat.
“School tomorrow is enough for now. Rest, Curtis-kun. Please.”
She did not ask him to stay on the line longer. The trust was still too newly patched for that. But the way she said tomorrow held less fear than it had an hour earlier — and more of the careful hope that the fracture might still be repairable if both of them showed up to it awake.
Curtis’s grip tightened around the new phone until the edge bit into his palm.
Long black braids hung forward against the gray hoodie as the full shape of her evening settled over him properly — hours of searching, unanswered calls into a drowned line, the fear that she had fractured something, all while he sat on ledges and ordered people out of rooms and pretended isolation was still a functional structure. The apology felt too small for the time she had spent aiming worry at an empty city. The address felt too late. I’ll make it up to you somehow sat in his chest like a promise made by a man who was not sure he knew how to stop creating the damage in the first place.
He stared at the dark TV screen across the suite and forced the last of the conversation into something he could actually complete.
“…Yeah. School.” A short, rough pause. “Good night, Anri-chan.”
He did not trust himself with another sentence. Anything longer risked turning into another confession, another retreat, or another poorly aimed kindness that would only make her search the streets again. The call ended softly under his thumb.
In the quiet that followed, Curtis remained upright on the couch with the phone still hard in his hand, the restored contact list glowing faintly, and the awful weight of being cared for by people he kept teaching — by accident and by habit — how badly that care could cost them.
The new phone lit up again before the residual quiet from Anri’s call had fully settled.
Rio.
Curtis stared at the name for a second, long black braids shifting against the gray hoodie as a thin, humorless breath left him. Of course. The restored line was doing its job with no interest in pacing or mercy. He tipped his head back against the couch and muttered toward the empty suite,
“…Well. At least she didn’t show up at my door when Celty was here.”
The alternative arranged itself too easily: Rio in the hallway, Celty still inside, blood on the floorboards, a white scythe’s afterimage still hanging in the air, and one more person discovering how many versions of him could occupy the same night. The joke landed dry and private, then died. He answered before the third ring.
“…Hey, Rio.”
On the other end, her voice came through immediate and unguarded, the particular mix of relief and residual worry that belonged to someone who had been trying a drowned number for hours and only now got a living answer.
“Curtis? Finally. Do you have any idea how many times I called?” A short breath, less angry than strained. “You just went dark. No ring, no reply, nothing. I was starting to think something actually happened to you.”
A beat, softer and more directly honest:
“…Are you okay? And don’t do that vague thing. I’ve been staring at an unanswered screen half the evening.”
Curtis shut his eyes, long black braids shifting against the gray hoodie as one more possible consequence of the afternoon arranged itself with ugly clarity.
“…Don’t tell me I went viral off that fight…”
Rio’s answer came without mercy or delay.
“You did.” A short, incredulous breath. “Curtis, there are already multiple angles up. The walk-out song, the part where he hits you and nothing happens, the knee, the lift, the—” she cut herself off, then pushed through it anyway, “—the ending. People are calling you the Chief of the North like the showman named a new local cryptid. Someone even stitched it with that busking clip from when your hood fell down.”
A thinner beat, less gossip and more concern.
“It’s not just random street-fight noise either. It’s spreading through the school boards and the local forums. People are asking who the tall foreigner with the braids is. Some of them already think you’re not normal.” Her voice tightened. “I only found out because the video hit one of the group chats. Then when I couldn’t reach you at all, it started looking a lot worse than a dumb viral moment.”
On the couch, Curtis tipped his head back and stared at the dark ceiling of the suite like the building itself might offer an exit from public narrative.
The Chief of the North.
The busking foreigner.
The man who did not fall when he was hit.
The city was already doing what cities did best: taking a messy afternoon and turning it into a story that no longer required his consent.
Curtis let out a thin breath and tipped the new phone against his temple, long black braids shifting against the gray hoodie as the practical absurdity of the situation finally found a dry little exit valve.
“…Greeaaat. And there’s no refund for the college fees. I’m all paid up. It would be a waste to run.”
The joke landed flat and self-aware, the kind of darkly practical humor a man used when the alternative was admitting the city had already started building a myth out of his worst public hour.
On the other end, Rio was quiet for half a second — then huffed a sound that was almost a laugh and almost not.
“…Don’t even joke about running.” A short pause, softer but pointed. “I just spent half the evening wondering if something happened to you. The last thing I need is you turning a viral street fight into a transfer-student disappearance bit.”
Another beat, more honest under the scold.
“Also, for the record, paid-up tuition is a terrible reason to stay in a city that’s already turning you into content. Stay because you want to. Stay because the people here aren’t only the ones filming you.” Her voice tightened a fraction. “Are you actually okay, or are you doing that thing where you make a joke so nobody checks the real answer?”
In the suite, Curtis’s mouth twitched despite himself — not quite a smile, more the resigned recognition that Rio had never been easy to misdirect once she decided to aim directly.
Curtis dragged a hand down his face, long black braids shifting against the gray hoodie as Rio’s directness cut cleanly through the last of his convenient fog.
“…Well. You got me, Rio. I’m deflecting again.” A short pause. “Yeah. I’m fine though.”
“I saw Anri in the video,” Rio said, not letting the fine become the whole story. “Is that why you couldn’t hang out today?” A thinner beat, late-evening honesty pushing past caution. “I know it’s late, but… I can come over tonight.”
Curtis looked at the floorboards he had already cleaned once, then at the suite that had held Celty, a white scythe, blood, and a tower’s aftermath in the same stretch of hours. The place was no longer actively disastrous. It was also not ready for another person to walk into without carrying the residue of everything he had not explained.
“…Uhh. My house is not in order at the moment. But it’s late, Rio. We have class in the morning.”
“Then I can stay the night,” she answered, simple and decided.
Curtis shut his eyes.
“…Yeah. If you want.”
“Great. I’ll get ready and text you.”
The line went dead.
For one full second Curtis remained frozen on the couch with the new phone in his hand and the fresh consequence of his own inability to say no sitting in the quiet like a live wire. Then white smoke surged off his shoulders in controlled sheets, racing across the suite in accelerated practical panic — cushions straightened, residual traces erased, surfaces wiped, the last evidence of crisis folded out of immediate sight with supernatural efficiency. The work of twenty careful minutes collapsed into a blur of pale vapor and movement.
When it cleared, the flat looked merely lived-in instead of post-disaster.
Curtis stood in the middle of it, breathing harder than the cleaning required, mismatched eyes wide with self-reproach.
“…Why. Why? Why do I do this. I should just say no and drink alone.”
From somewhere that was not the room and not exactly memory either, Justice’s voice arrived like a brother leaning on the doorframe of the afterlife with both eyebrows raised.
Because you’re allergic to leaving people unanswered, dumbass.
A warm, familiar laugh ghosted under the words.
Phone in the lake, tower jump, white scythe, blood on the floor — and your big recovery plan is letting another girl come spend the night because she asked nicely and you forgot how the word no works. Classic. If I still had a body, I’d be taking bets on how long it takes you to realize you just scheduled emotional continuity for 8 a.m. classes.
Curtis tipped his head back toward the ceiling.
Justice kept going, delighted and merciless in equal measure.
Also, for the record: ‘my house is not in order’ is not a no. ‘We have class in the morning’ is not a no. ‘Yeah if you want’ is the opposite of a no. You’re going to die of attachment long before anything on that list finishes you. And I’m going to haunt every second of it.
Curtis dropped back onto the couch, long black braids spilling over the gray hoodie as he stared at the ceiling like it might offer a better operating system than the one he was stuck with.
“…Justice, what would you do?” A short, self-aware breath. “Probably already be in an entanglement with those girls and more.”
The answer came immediately, warm with afterlife smugness.
Oh, absolutely.
Justice’s presence settled into the suite like a brother who had never needed a chair to occupy a room.
Let’s be honest. If I were still walking around with a pulse, Rio would already have a key, Anri would be convinced I was reformable, and Celty would be having the worst week of her immortal life because I’d be on her motorcycle seat asking philosophical questions at 2 a.m. I’d be entangled, compromised, deeply unwise, and having the time of my life while you stood off to the side muttering about lists and landlord fees.
A softer, sharper turn entered the voice.
But I’m not there. So congratulations. You’re doing it in my place anyway — just with worse technique and more self-loathing.
Curtis’s mouth tightened.
Justice kept going, half prophecy and half affectionate indictment.
You’re going to keep answering them. You’re going to keep opening doors you swear you’re not ready for. Rio’s on her way over right now because you said ‘yeah if you want’ like a man with no survival instincts. Anri has your address. Celty has already seen the white scythe and the blood and still didn’t stay gone. You’re not avoiding entanglement, Curtis. You’re just collecting it with extra guilt and better braids.
A faint laugh ghosted through the quiet flat.
So if you’re asking what I would do? I’d lean in, make the mess louder, and refuse to drown a phone over it. Since I’m stuck narrating from the cheap seats… I predict you end up doing the same thing slower, with more tragic pauses, and somehow still acting surprised every time another person refuses to leave you alone.
Curtis covered his face with one hand.
“…I hate that your spirit is somewhere probly talking shit about my situation.”
I know, Justice said cheerfully. That’s why it still counts as brotherly support.
The teasing residue of Justice’s voice still hung in the suite when Curtis’s hand fell away from his face. Long black braids shifted against the gray hoodie as he stared at the empty air across from the couch — at the place a living brother would have occupied if the list, the forest, and a final cruel decision had not taken him first.
“…I miss you, Justice.” The words came out low, stripped of performance. “Every day.”
For a second there was no joke waiting in answer. No prophecy about girls at the door. No commentary on drowned phones or white scythes. Just the quieter frequency of a presence that had been listening longer than it always admitted.
Me too, man.
A short pause, rougher than the earlier laughter.
I’m sorry.
There was no expansion after it. No clever recovery. No second bit to make the apology safer. The suite simply held the two lines — I miss you every day and me too, I’m sorry — until even the shape of Justice’s voice began to thin, like a signal finally giving out under its own weight.
Then the narration cut off.
No fade joke.
No final jab from the cheap seats.
Just absence, sudden and complete, as if the dead had been allowed one clean moment of honesty and used it up.
The knock came just as the last of Justice’s absence settled into the suite.
Curtis stood, long black braids shifting against the gray hoodie, and did a quick visual sweep of the living room before answering — surfaces clear, no blood, no residual crisis staged in plain sight, white smoke already dispersed into nothing useful. Only then did he open the door.
Rio stood in the hallway with a bag over one shoulder and the particular alertness of someone who had walked through an active aftermath on the way up.
“Was there an accident?” she asked immediately, dark eyes searching his face and then the corridor behind her. “Cops were talking to a couple down the street and some guy was being hauled off in cuffs toward a hospital. Not to mention the very expensive-looking crack in the pavement outside your flat.”
Curtis leaned one shoulder into the doorframe, mismatched eyes half-lidded in a carefully ordinary expression.
“…Yeah. Some idiot tried to rob an old couple near the lot. Things got messy. The pavement’s been like that.” A short, dry pause as he stepped aside to let her in. “Landlord’s going to have a stroke when he sees the bill. Come in before the hallway starts charging rent for this conversation.”
He did not mention the roof.
He did not mention the bottle-turned-missile.
He did not mention the landing that had put the spiderweb crack there in the first place.
Rio crossed the threshold, still glancing once more toward the street-facing side of the building as if the cracked asphalt might offer a clearer story than the tall foreigner holding the door. Behind her, Curtis shut them in, the new phone heavy in his pocket and the carefully restored flat suddenly occupied again — proof that saying yeah if you want always had consequences, and tonight those consequences had arrived with questions already aimed at the damage outside.
Curtis shut the door behind her and tipped his head toward the living room, long black braids shifting against the gray hoodie.
“Come in. Make yourself comfy. I was actually going to shower.”
Rio took two steps in, then stopped, nose wrinkling as the residual night air coming off him finally registered properly.
“…You smell like alcohol.”
Curtis blinked, then chuckled under his breath, mismatched eyes half-lidded with dry surprise.
“Still? Wow.”
Rio crossed her arms, bag still on her shoulder, expression caught between concern and the particular irritation of someone who had spent hours failing to reach him only to arrive and find the aftermath on his clothes.
“Yes, still. And don’t ‘wow’ me like that’s impressive.” A short pause as her gaze moved over him more carefully — the tired posture, the cleaned flat, the man who had gone viral, gone dark, and then answered the door like the evening were only mildly inconvenient. “If you were drinking enough that it’s still on you when I get here, that kind of matters after the day you had. Fight video. No answer. Now this.”
Another quieter beat, less scold than direct honesty.
“…Are you actually fine, or did you just clean up enough to pass a doorway inspection?” She set her bag down by the couch without looking away from him. “Go shower if you need to. But I’m not going to pretend alcohol-on-your-clothes and a cracked pavement crime scene outside are just random flavor for the night.”
Curtis’s mouth pulled into a small, defeated smile, long black braids shifting against the gray hoodie as Rio dismantled the doorway version of him with upsetting efficiency.
“…Oh shit…”
There was no real defense under it. Just the quiet recognition that whatever white-smoke cleaning and casual tone he had prepared were not going to survive contact with someone who had already spent the evening staring at unanswered calls and fight videos.
Rio’s eyes narrowed, not unkindly, but with the firmness of a girl who refused to let oh shit become the whole explanation.
“…Yeah. ‘Oh shit.’” A short pause. “That is exactly the face of a man who thought he could open the door, make a joke, and have me not notice the rest.” She tipped her head toward the living room, then back to him. “Go shower. I’ll wait. But when you come back out, I want a real version of the afternoon — not the landlord-and-pavement summary, and not the paid-up-tuition bit.”
Another quieter beat, softer around the edges.
“I’m already here, Curtis. You don’t have to pass inspection. You just have to stop acting like the cleaned-up version is the only one I’m allowed to sit with.”
Curtis leaned back against the counter, long black braids shifting against the gray hoodie as a tired, crooked edge of honesty entered his voice.
“…I’m glad you’re here.” A short pause as he tipped the glass slightly. “Now you get to see how I live and function at school — ’cause I’m not sleeping.”
It landed half as joke, half as warning label. The calendar bottle, the quiet-night plan, the restored phone, the residual alcohol — all of it suddenly framed by the more permanent fact underneath: this was not a one-off restless evening. This was the operating system. Night held him upright. Morning classes got whatever was left after that.
Rio studied him for a second, the cleaned flat and the marked calendar rearranging themselves around that admission.
“…So this is the version that shows up to Raira,” she said quietly. “No sleep. Drink on the schedule. Still somehow standing through lectures and everything else.”
A shorter beat, less judgment than recalibration.
“I’m glad you said you’re glad I’m here. That part matters.” Her fingers rested against the counter near the empty second glass. “But if you’re telling me up front that you’re not sleeping, then I’m not going to spend the night pretending this is just one weird Thursday. If this is how you actually function… I want to see it honestly, not the cleaned-up doorway edition.”
Another softer line:
“Just don’t use me being here as an excuse to push the no-sleep thing farther than you already were. I’m company, Curtis. Not an audience for self-destruction.”
Curtis shook his head once, long black braids shifting against the gray hoodie as the line landed with more weight than the rest of the evening’s careful talk. His mismatched eyes held hers without the earlier dry deflection.
“I would never put that on you, Rio. That would be cruel… and go against what I told you that night I caught you.”
The suite seemed to quiet around the reference.
Not the viral fight. Not the calendar bottle. The original night — the rooftop, the jump, the foreigner who had refused to watch her disappear and then told her, plain and steady, that her life was still hers to keep. Whatever messy architecture had filled the days since, that first promise still sat under his voice like a fixed point he refused to violate.
Rio’s expression changed at once.
The ready concern, the half-scolding edge, the practical alertness from the cracked pavement and the alcohol on his clothes — all of it slipped for a second under something more exposed. Her fingers tightened against the counter. For a moment she only looked at him, the tall figure in the gray hoodie who had once pulled her out of open air and was now standing in a kitchen insisting he would not use her presence as a stage for his own collapse.
“…Curtis,” she got out, quieter than before.
A short breath left her, unsteady around the edges.
“You remember it that clearly.” Not quite a question. A realization landing warm and heavy at once. “Most people would use a night like that as leverage, or forget the exact shape of what they said once the emergency was over. And you’re standing here using it as a reason not to put your worst habits on me.”
Her eyes flicked down, then back up, softer and more serious.
“…Good.” The word was small and firm. “Because that night still matters to me. What you said still matters. If you’re going to keep any version of yourself in line, I’m glad it’s that one.” A thinner pause. “Just… keep meaning it. Even on the days that already have a bottle marked on the calendar.”
Curtis paused mid-motion, long black braids shifting against the gray hoodie as he caught the look she gave the glass and the untouched second one beside it.
“…Wow. Not a big fan of alcohol?”
Rio shook her head, a little self-conscious but direct.
“I’ve never drank.”
That was enough.
Curtis set his glass down, found a funnel in a drawer with the unthinking efficiency of a man who lived alone and knew his own kitchen, and poured the liquor carefully back into the fancy bottle. No speech about it. No performance of sacrifice. Just the quiet correction of a host who refused to keep drinking in front of someone for whom the whole ritual meant nothing but distance. He capped the bottle, put it away, and disappeared into the bedroom long enough to come back with a towel over one shoulder.
“Shower time.”
Twenty minutes later the bathroom fan had gone quiet and Curtis re-emerged in clean dark clothes, damp braids freshly re-tied, the residual street-alcohol smell finally gone from him. He looked more human for the wash — still tired, still carrying the day, but no longer wearing the fight and the bottle on his skin.
Rio had settled on the couch by then, bag set aside, phone face-down, the cleaned suite no longer feeling quite as much like a doorway inspection. When she looked up at him, some of the earlier sharpness had eased into something warmer and more openly relieved.
“…Better,” she said simply.
A short pause as her gaze moved over the damp braids and the fact that the drink had gone back into the bottle without argument.
“You didn’t have to pour it out for me. Or back in. Whatever.” A smaller breath, honest. “But I noticed. And I noticed you remembered the night on the roof well enough to use it as a reason not to put the ugly version of the evening on me.”
She tipped her head toward the other end of the couch, leaving room without demanding it.
“You look less like a crime scene now. Sit. If you’re really not sleeping, then at least stop standing in your own kitchen like you’re still deciding whether I’m allowed to see the real layout of the place.”
Curtis sat down beside her on the couch, long damp braids shifting against his clean shirt as the last of the shower-warmth still clung to him. He left a little space at first, then settled more honestly once the quiet accepted him there.
“…My bad. I didn’t mean to make you uncomfortable.” A short, plain pause. “You could’ve said something sooner. I would not have minded.”
Rio glanced sideways at him, the earlier tension around the bottle and the viral afternoon softening under the simpler apology. For a second she only studied the damp braids, the cleaner version of him, the fact that the drink had gone back into the bottle without any sulking attached to it.
“…I know,” she said. “That’s the problem, kind of.”
A short breath left her, more honest than sharp.
“You’re the type who actually would stop if someone said they were uncomfortable. I could tell that even before you grabbed the funnel.” Her fingers rested loosely against her knee. “I just… didn’t want to walk into your place after hours of unanswered calls and immediately start managing your night. Especially when you’d already marked the bottle on the calendar and looked like you’d been through more than you were saying.”
Another quieter beat as she tipped her head toward him.
“I’m not fragile about other people drinking. I’ve just never done it, and the way the evening started — fight video, silence, alcohol on your clothes — made the glass look heavier than a normal pour.” She met his eyes more directly. “You didn’t do anything wrong by having it out. I should’ve just said so earlier instead of letting you read it off my face.”
A smaller, warmer edge entered her voice.
“For the record… putting it back once you knew was enough. You don’t have to keep apologizing for a drink you never tried to push on me.”
Curtis rested his elbows loosely on his knees, long damp braids shifting as he looked at her without any of the earlier defensive edge.
“The way you kept mentioning it and your body language read as uncomfortable.” A short, plain pause. “I don’t think you’re fragile. I think it’s rude to make your guests uncomfortable.”
The distinction landed cleanly — not protection of someone he assumed couldn’t handle a drink, but basic host principle. The bottle had gone back because she was in his home, not because he thought she needed managing.
Rio’s expression eased further, a small breath leaving her like something had finally been named in the right shape.
“…Okay. That actually helps.”
A shorter beat as she angled toward him on the couch, less guarded than when she’d first come through the door.
“I didn’t want you thinking I was judging the whole night or trying to police what you do in your own place. Especially after showing up late and already asking about cracked pavement and unanswered calls.” Her fingers relaxed against her knee. “But yeah. The glass felt like one more heavy thing in a room that already had too many. The fact that you read that and corrected it without turning it into a thing… that’s why I said putting it back was enough.”
Another quieter line, warmer around the edges.
“For what it’s worth, I don’t feel like a guest you’re performing for right now. That matters more than the bottle ever did.” She tipped her head slightly. “So thank you. For noticing. And for not making me explain the discomfort like it was some big moral stance.”
Curtis gave a small nod, long damp braids shifting against his clean shirt as the last of the bottle-tension left the space between them.
“No worries.”
Rio accepted that for a second, then shifted on the couch, angling more fully toward him now that the immediate defensiveness had thinned. The cracked pavement, the viral clip, the hours of unanswered calls, and the man sitting beside her in freshly washed clothes were still all in the same room. Comfortable or not, she hadn’t come over just to watch him be polite.
“…Okay. Then can I ask a few things without you treating it like an interrogation?”
Curtis’s mismatched eyes flicked toward her, tired but open enough. He didn’t say no.
Rio took that as permission.
“First — after the fight, when you went dark. Was that only because of the way Anri looked at you, or was there more stacked on top of it?” A short pause, careful. “You don’t have to give me every detail. I just don’t want to keep filling in the blank hours with worse versions than the truth.”
She held up a second finger, softer.
“Second — when you say you’re not sleeping, is that a tonight problem or a regular one? Because you said it like I was about to see the real operating system, not one bad night.”
A third, quieter beat.
“And third…” Her voice lowered, more personal than practical. “Are you actually glad I’m here, or did you say yes because you’re bad at closing the door on people who reach for you after a rough day?”
She left the questions there, not stacked like accusations, just three honest points of pressure in a cleaned flat that still smelled faintly of soap and whatever the day had done to him before she knocked.
Curtis turned the empty space of his hands over once on his knees, long damp braids shifting as he answered without the earlier deflection.
“Yeah. It was the way she looked at me. I felt disappointed in myself. Like I went too far… and felt like a freak.”
A short pause. His mismatched eyes stayed on the floor a second longer before the next answer.
“I have had insomnia since I was young. As far as I know, it does not affect my health.”
Then, quieter, more careful around the third point:
“I am glad. Because when you looked at me when you walked in… it wasn’t the same as the way Anri looked at me earlier.”
Rio absorbed all three without rushing to fix them into something prettier. For a moment she only sat with the shape of what he’d actually said — not just that the stare hurt, but that it had turned inward into self-disappointment and that old, ugly word: freak. The insomnia admission landed almost practically by comparison. The last answer, though, warmed something in her chest even as it made the responsibility of being the safer pair of eyes feel suddenly real.
“…Okay,” she said softly.
A short breath left her.
“I’m glad you told me it was specifically that look. Not the crowd. Not the video. The fact that someone who matters to you stared like you’d crossed out of the category where she still recognized you.” Her fingers rested loosely against the couch cushion between them. “That would make anyone want to disappear. Especially someone who’s already used to being watched wrong by strangers.”
Another quieter beat.
“And for the record — when I walked in, I was worried. I was annoyed about the unanswered calls. I was thrown by the cops and the cracked pavement. But I wasn’t looking at you like you’d stopped being Curtis.” She met his eyes more directly. “You’re still you. Even after a fight that went too far. Even after a night with a bottle marked on the calendar. Even after making people panic by going dark.”
A smaller, firmer line:
“If my face at the door helped even a little… good. Just don’t use that as permission to keep testing how bad a friend’s eyes can get before you decide the only option is vanishing again.”
She tipped her head slightly, softer around the edges.
“I’m here. That can be enough for tonight without turning into another performance of okay.”
Curtis leaned back into the couch, long damp braids shifting against his clean shirt as some of the hardness finally left his shoulders. When he looked at Rio again, the tiredness was still there, but the edge of self-judgment had thinned into something more openly sincere.
“…Thanks, Rio. I mean it.” A short pause. “You didn’t need to come all this way to ask or say that. But I appreciate it dearly.”
Rio’s expression softened at once, the last of the doorway tension easing into something quieter and more settled. For a second she only watched him — the boy who had caught her on a roof, gone viral in a street fight, vanished for hours, and was now thanking her like basic concern were an unexpected gift.
“…I know I didn’t need to,” she said. “That’s kind of why I came.”
A smaller breath left her, honest and unguarded.
“You went dark after a day that already had too many eyes on you. I wasn’t going to sit across town guessing whether you were fine just because the alternative was inconvenient.” Her fingers relaxed against the cushion between them. “You don’t have to treat people showing up as some rare debt either. If I care enough to call that many times, I care enough to walk a few blocks and say the true version out loud.”
Another quieter beat, warmer around the edges.
“You’re welcome, Curtis. Just… keep meaning the part where you appreciate it instead of using the gratitude as a polite way to close the subject.” She tipped her head slightly. “I’m already here. You can let that be simple for a minute.”
Rio shifted on the couch, long enough into the quieter stretch of the evening for curiosity to start testing the edges of safer subjects. Her voice came out almost casual, though her eyes gave her away a little.
“…Have you had a girlfriend? Or kissed a girl?”
Curtis raised an eyebrow, long damp braids shifting against his clean shirt as the question landed somewhere between unexpected and inevitable.
“Yeah. And yeah.” A short pause. “Before I came here I had one, but… that’s something I don’t wanna get into.”
Rio blinked, then softened at the boundary.
“Oh. I’m sorry. Do you miss her?”
Curtis chuckled under his breath, the sound dry and immediate, mismatched eyes half-lidded with zero nostalgia.
“Fuck no.”
Rio’s face brightened before she could fully hide it. The relief was small, unpolished, and completely honest — a quick lift in her expression, a breath that came out almost like a laugh she hadn’t planned to release. Whatever careful neutrality she might have prepared for a more complicated answer dissolved on contact with that blunt fuck no.
“…Good,” she said, then caught herself and tried to sound less openly pleased about it. Failed a little. “I mean — good that you’re not sitting here broken up over someone back wherever. That would make tonight heavier than it already was.”
A shorter beat, warmer and less successfully casual.
“Also, for the record, I’m not sorry I asked if the answer was that easy.” Her mouth curved despite the attempt at composure. “Fuck no is a very clear emotional status.”
Curtis chuckled again, low and tired-warm, long damp braids shifting as he caught the way Rio had failed to hide how much she liked the answer. The sound had no edge in it this time — just the quiet amusement of a man watching someone try to walk back a reaction that had already given her away.
Rio’s cheeks colored a little at being caught so easily. She clicked her tongue, then gave up on pretending the moment had been purely informational.
“…Don’t chuckle at me like that. You dropped fuck no like a brick and expected me to keep a straight face.” A short pause, still smiling despite herself. “After the day you had, hearing you’re not pining over some leftover girlfriend from before Japan is allowed to improve the room. That’s all.”
She tipped her head toward him, softer and more teasing around the edges.
“Besides. If you didn’t want a reaction, you should’ve given a boring answer.”
Curtis’s mouth tugged into a small, genuine smile, long damp braids shifting against his clean shirt.
“That’s fair. Ahaha.”
After that, the night finally became what he had marked on the calendar in a better shape than planned — not a bottle and an empty suite, just quiet. They talked in lower pieces, let silences exist without turning them into tests, and let the cleaned flat hold something almost ordinary after a day that had been anything but. No more interrogation. No more viral-fight replay. Just two people sitting through the late hour until class tomorrow became a real boundary again.
Eventually Rio stood and collected her bag.
“I don’t have my school clothes on me.” A short, slightly shy pause at the door. “Next time… could I spend the night?”
Curtis nodded without making it complicated.
“Of course, Rio.”
She lingered one second longer, dark eyes softer than when she had first arrived asking about cops and cracked pavement.
“Good night, Curtis-kun.”
Then she was gone.
The hallway emptied. The door clicked shut. The suite returned to its single-occupant quiet, though it no longer felt quite as stripped as it had after Celty left and the phone first rang. Curtis remained on the couch a moment longer, looking at the closed door, and answered the empty air with the same gentleness she had given him on the way out.
